<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranbir Singh of Jammu and Kashmir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ranbir Singh was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> maharaja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> in 1857. He supported the British in 1857, extended control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a>, and issued a law code known as the Ranbir Penal Code. He also patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning and built temples in Jammu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranbir-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir.jpg" alt="Ranbir Singh of Jammu and Kashmir" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Calcutta, Bombay &amp;amp; Simla : Bourne &amp;amp; Shepherd (active 1864-1900s) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharajah_Ranbir_Singh_of_Jammu_and_Kashmir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
